<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/revolutions-of-1848">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cambridge’s Option A core opens with exactly this lesson’s question: <strong>Were the
    revolutions of 1848 important?</strong> In eighteen months, almost every capital between
    Paris and Vienna erupted — the “springtime of peoples”. As a series of seizures of power,
    1848 was a catastrophe: nearly every revolution was defeated within two years. As a
    turning point, it is hard to overstate: <strong>democracy, nationalism and the method of
    unifying states from above all date their modern careers to this failure</strong>. The
    syllabus asks you to hold both truths — causes, events and results in France, Italy,
    Germany and the Austrian Empire, and an honest verdict on what failed and what didn’t.
    The five key questions give the page its spine: why liberalism and nationalism had grown
    so strong by 1848; why the revolutions came all at once; what they had in common; why
    most failed; and what changed anyway. Hold the two truths together and the verdict
    writes itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the revolution, month by month — use the chips to compare the four theatres" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <p>
    Drag the scrubber slowly. What jumps out is <em>simultaneity</em>: within six weeks of
    February, barricades were up in four empires. That is no accident — the same bad harvests,
    the same ideas, and news travelling by railway and telegraph made 1848 a single European
    event wearing local costumes.
  </p>

  <h2>Why liberalism and nationalism had grown by 1848</h2>
  <p>
    Two creeds had been building for thirty years under the Congress system
    designed to bury them. <strong>Liberalism</strong> was the creed of the
    century's new men — lawyers, professors, merchants, doctors — and its
    programme was specific: written constitutions limiting rulers, free
    press and end to censorship, jury trial, careers open to talent rather
    than birth, and property qualifications framed as rights.
    Industrialisation fed it: railways, factories and a commercial press created a
    literate, travelling, petitioning middle class that local absolutism
    could not silence — censorship merely kept its grievances fresh.
    <strong>Nationalism</strong> answered a different question — who is "the
    people" that liberal rights belong to? — and answered it with language
    and culture: Herder's Volksgeist, folk epics collected, dictionaries
    compiled, the German gymnasts, Czech revivalists, Hungarian
    language-laws. The 1820s (Spain, Naples, Greece), 1830 (France, Belgium,
    Poland) and 1846 (Kraków, Galicia) risings were its rehearsals; each
    failed and each taught. The two creeds were natural allies — both
    opposed the dynasts and the 1815 settlement — but their alliance had a
    fault line running straight through the multi-national empires: whose
    nation governs in Bohemia, Hungary, the Balkans? In 1848 the fault
    line would open underfoot.
  </p>

  <h2>Why so many revolutions at once: the economic fuse</h2>
  <p>
    Ideas explain the demands; the harvest explains the timing. From
    <strong>1845 the potato blight</strong> (the same organism as Ireland's
    famine) destroyed the poor's staple across northern Europe, and
    <strong>1846–47 grain failures</strong> sent food prices to double their
    normal levels in many cities — bread riots were already the routine
    of 1847 in Berlin and Vienna. Then the <strong>financial and industrial
    slump of 1847</strong> threw artisans out of work precisely when bread
    cost most: the hungry crowd, the classic eighteenth-century
    urban threat, was back on the streets with a nineteenth-century
    political vocabulary. Add the accelerants: railways carried news
    (and strikers) faster than cavalry; telegraph wires carried
    Paris's outcome to Vienna in days; newspapers were the
    revolution's gramophone. When Paris's February rising dissolved
    a Bourbon monarchy in seventy-two hours — proof of concept, as
    every capital read it — the calculation every government faced
    was identical: concede now or gamble the throne. Most conceded:
    constitutions in Prussia and the Austrian lands, press freedom
    everywhere, national parliaments summoned in Frankfurt and
    Budapest. The counter-attack then took two years to reconquer
    what had been surrendered in two weeks — which is why the
    year's outcome, liberty everywhere and its defeat nearly
    everywhere, is so confusable until you keep the timeline
    in front of you.
  </p>

  <h2>What they had in common: the three-act pattern</h2>
  <p>
    Strip the local costumes and 1848 repeats a script. <strong>Act one —
    the rising:</strong> barricades (that revolutionary technology of the
    1830s Parisian streets, exported in memory), National Guard mutinies,
    a palace concession. <strong>Act two — the constitutional moment:</strong>
    liberals legislate — Frenchmen found the Second Republic with
    universal male suffrage (the first in Europe since 1793) and
    "national workshops" for the unemployed; Germans elect the
    Frankfurt Parliament to the Paulskirche; Hungarians win the
    April Laws from Vienna. <strong>Act three — the split:</strong> the
    question the revolution had deferred — what does the new order
    owe the poor? — detonates the coalition. In Paris the answer
    came in June: the government closed the national workshops, some
    100,000 workers rose, and <strong>General Cavaignac crushed them in
    the streets of the republic that had promised them work</strong> —
    1,500 dead in four days, thousands deported. The June Days are
    1848's hinge and its symbol: the "springtime of peoples" had
    split into two peoples, and after June the propertied classes
    everywhere began asking the old regimes for protection —
    Marx's point in the <em>Eighteenth Brumaire</em>: the
    bourgeois revolution had ended against its own
    sans-culottes.
  </p>

  <h2>Why they failed — five reasons, ranked</h2>
  <p>
    First, <strong>the revolutions cancelled each other</strong>. In the
    multi-national empires, nationalism turned on nationalism:
    German liberals wanted a greater Germany including Bohemia;
    Czech nationalism (Palacký's letter refusing Frankfurt —
    "I am a Czech of Slav blood") wanted neither; in Hungary,
    the Magyar revolution under Kossuth alienated Croats, Serbs
    and Romanians, who fought <em>for</em> Vienna against Budapest.
    The minorities feared each other's nation-state more than the
    emperor's empire. Second, <strong>the armies held</strong>. The decisive
    fact of 1848 is that the soldiers of Prussia and Austria —
    after terrible hesitations in March — obeyed the courts:
    Windischgrätz bombarded Prague (June) and stormed Vienna
    (October 31). Third, <strong>foreign intervention</strong>: Tsar
    Nicholas I, the "gendarme of Europe", sent some
    <strong>200,000 troops</strong> into Hungary in June 1849; the
    Hungarian army broke at Világos on 13 August. French troops
    restored Pope Pius IX in Rome (July 1849). The dynasties'
    survival was a coalition achievement. Fourth,
    <strong>constitutionalism without power</strong>: the Frankfurt
    Parliament spent its year on the philosophical definition
    of German (whether to include Austrian Germans) and its
    famous Fundamental Rights, while the princes reassembled
    armies; its offer of the imperial crown to Frederick William
    IV (April 1849) was refused — the king would not accept
    "a crown from the gutter" — and the deputies dispersed to
    be arrested or exiled. Fifth, <strong>the rural majority was
    never mobilised</strong>: except where abolition was on offer,
    peasants watched urban politics as spectators, and the
    June Days taught liberals to fear their own poor.
    Add the parts and the failure is overdetermined — which
    is exactly why it taught so clearly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the people" rose as one against "the
  rulers" and were beaten. The revolutions were coalitions — middle-class
  liberals, urban artisans, peasants, students, nationalities — that
  defeated the regimes, then dissolved against each other: Paris's June
  Days, Bohemia's Czechs versus Frankfurt's Germans, Hungary's Magyars
  versus its own Croats. What 1848 lacked was not numbers but a
  coalition that could hold. (Its politics was also entirely male:
  women's clubs were banned within months in Paris, and the
  Paulskirche voted women's political rights out of its
  Fundamental Rights — a revolution with a ceiling.)</p>

  <h2>What changed anyway: the verdict on importance</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's last question is where the essay earns its mark, and the
    changes fall into three classes. <strong>Concessions that stuck:</strong>
    <strong>universal male suffrage in France</strong> — the December 1848
    election of Louis-Napoleon (nearly three-quarters of the vote,
    a peasant mandate) proved democracy durable and dangerous; the
    Second Republic's constitution survived its own emperor's coup
    (1851) as the template of European politics. <strong>Serfdom in the
    Habsburg lands</strong>, abolished September 1848, was never restored:
    the Austrian state learned to rule a free peasantry — and peasants,
    given their land, never again rebelled <em>for</em> urban liberals.
    Prussia kept a constitution and an elected Landtag (with the
    notorious three-class franchise — but the principle of constitutional
    government was now the price of legitimacy). <strong>Lessons that
    governed the next twenty years:</strong> Cavour drew the Italian
    moral — unification needs a great-power ally and a dynastic
    engine, not barricades (1859–61); Bismarck drew the German one
    — "not by speeches and majority votes… but by iron and blood"
    (1862), and built the nation from above in three wars (1864–71).
    <strong>Long-run currents:</strong> the political vocabulary of mass
    democracy (franchise, constitution, national self-determination)
    entered European politics permanently; the failed revolutionaries
    — the <em>Forty-Eighters</em> — emigrated by the tens of thousands
    (Carl Schurz's America, Garibaldi's legend, Mazzini's networks),
    carrying nationalism abroad; and the working class, burned by
    June, began organising separately — the trade-union and socialist
    movements of the 1860s are June Days' children. Hobsbawm's
    verdict is the standard one: 1848 ended the "dual revolution"'s
    first act and opened its second — as a defeat, it was the
    century's most productive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the arc of 1848 — rise, pattern, failure, and the changes that stuck" widget="equationStep" config={arcCfg} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The historiography of a "failure".</strong> The verdict on 1848 has
    swung with every generation: contemporaries wrote obituaries
    (the <em>Kreuzzeitung</em>'s sneer, "the fools' year"); Marx theorised
    the defeat in the <em>Eighteenth Brumaire</em> (1852) — history
    repeating "first as tragedy, then as farce"; the liberal
    nationalisms it lost were crowned by 1871, which re-cast 1848
    as prophecy rather than fiasco; and modern social historians
    (Rapport, Hobsbawm) read it as the moment when <em>politics</em>
    became mass politics — the first European year in which
    ordinary people were, everywhere at once, constitutional
    actors. The exam skill is to date your verdict's lens:
    "failure" from the barricades, "turning point" from the
    1870s, "democratic breakthrough" from ours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The simultaneity mechanism, in detail.</strong> How fast did the
    news actually travel? The electric telegraph linked Paris to
    Brussels, Berlin and Vienna within the decade; railway
    timetables moved special editions and political refugees in
    days; and the panic of 1847 had already synchronised European
    credit markets. Historians argue how much weight to give the
    media versus the shared economic shock — the harvest did not
    need telegraph wires to be European. The residual puzzle is
    Britain's absence: the world's most industrialised society,
    with Chartism's sixth petition pending, stayed stable —
    the comparison (Parliament, cheap food policy shifting,
    policing that did not fire) is the standard counterfactual
    that explains what the continental regimes lacked.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How unified was "liberalism" really — was
    the Frankfurt Parliament a national body or a Prussian-weighted
    north German one? Would the Czechs, Poles and South Slavs have
    accepted a liberal Germany that had asked them rather than
    assumed them? And is "revolution" even the right frame, or is
    1848 better understood as Europe's first general election,
    fought with paving stones? The syllabus's five questions
    are answersable; the sixth — what 1848 <em>was</em> — is
    still being argued, which is the surest sign that
    the revolutions were important.
  </p>
